<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mons Graupius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">83 or 84</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-conquest-of-britain">Roman conquest of Britain</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Graupius was fought in 83 or 84 CE in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> between a Roman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a> and the Caledonians, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> names as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a>. Tacitus records a decisive Roman victory, but the site of the battle is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> did not follow it with a lasting conquest of the far north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius.jpg" alt="Battle of Mons Graupius" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agricola.Campaigns.80.84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, whose father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a> was Roman governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, is the source of nearly everything known of the battle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were often outnumbered in Britain but found it hard to make their enemies fight in open battle. The Caledonii were the last unconquered British tribe, and were never entirely subdued. After many years of avoiding battle they were forced to fight when the Romans marched on their main granaries just as the harvest had filled them, leaving them to fight or starve over the winter. Agricola had sent his fleet ahead to cause panic and reached the site with light infantry reinforced by British auxiliaries, finding it occupied by the enemy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>'s account the Roman centre was formed by 8,000 auxiliary infantry, with 3,000 cavalry on the flanks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> held in reserve in front of the camp. Estimates for the Roman army range from 17,000 to 30,000. The Caledonians, said to number over 30,000 and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a>, who is mentioned only as giving a speech that was probably fictitious, stood on rising ground in a horseshoe, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> on the plain below.</p><p>After a brief exchange of missiles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> sent forward auxiliaries, four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> of Batavians and two of Tungri swordsmen, in a frontal attack. The Caledonians were cut down on the lower slopes, and those who tried to outflank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were outflanked in turn by cavalry. The Caledonians fled to nearby woodland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> reports 10,000 Caledonian dead against 360 auxiliaries, and says 20,000 retreated into the woods and fared better there. Scouts could not find them next morning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The location is debated. In the 19th century it was linked with almost every Roman site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> and Kinross, but aerial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a> of marching camps has moved attention to the north-east, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bennachie">Bennachie</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a> and high ground near the Raedykes camp among the suggestions. Historic Environment Scotland excludes the battle from its inventory of battlefields because of this uncertainty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> in 96, and historians have long suspected exaggeration, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> was given triumphal honours. Soon after the battle he was recalled and replaced by Sallustius Lucullus. Within a few years Roman forces withdrew to the Forth-Clyde line, and Tacitus's remark that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was completely conquered and immediately let go reflects his disapproval of Domitian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mons Graupius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Vespasian the advance resumed, and Gnaeus Julius Agricola, governor from about 77 to 84, campaigned deep into Scotland and defeated the Caledonians at <b>Mons Graupius</b> in 83 or 84.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They first appear in history when the governor Agricola marched north, and Tacitus says a host of Caledonian peoples gathered under Calgacus to meet him at <b>Mons Graupius</b> in 83 or 84.</blockquote><a href="/state/caledonii">Caledonii</a></li></ul></section>
